ther  26 or 27 in in each false  in each fold  okay so we make now a matrix for our  errors and it's going to have  10 rows and 19 columns because they're  19 variables they're going to be 19  subsets  and 10 rows for each of the 10 folds  and so now we're going to go through a  double loop  and so we go for k equals 1 in 10  and now we fit a reg subsets model  with salary as a response  and the training data is going to be all  the observations whose fold id is not  equal to k  for the this kth fold  we're going to train on all the  observations but those in the kth fold  okay  so  having done that we only need to do that  once and now we're going to go and look  at each of the subsets in that trained  model so now we go through our loop for  i equals 1 to 19  and now we can use our predict method  that we just made  and when i say predict method i mean it  because we wrote our predict function in  a way that the generic predict  understands  and so  we just call predict  and the first argument is is best.fit  which is a reg subsets object and so  the generic function predict knows to  find the method which was called predict  subsets  and  now we're going to make predictions  at those observations whose folds fold  id is equal to k  right so those were the guys that were  left out  and we doing it for each subset size  i  which is given to our predict function  by id  okay so we make our predictions  and then we compute the mean squared  error of the predictions and assign that  into the kth row of our cv errors  and we do that for for all the  i and for all k and that's done already  well actually it's not  now it's done  so that was pretty quick that was fit in  19 no it was fit in 10 models so it's  not so bad but making predictions for 19  different sub-models of each  um we now process our output matrix so  first of all  we use the apply function to the columns  so we take the column means  right because there were 10 rows  and each row was the  mean squared 